Troubleshooting Tips
Snort won't start:

Check configuration syntax: snort -c /usr/local/etc/snort/snort.lua -T
Verify network interface exists: ip link show
Check file permissions on rules directory
Elasticsearch connection failed:

Verify service is running: sudo systemctl status elasticsearch
Check port availability: netstat -tlnp | grep 9200
Review logs: sudo tail -f /var/log/elasticsearch/ids-lab.log
No alerts generated:

Verify IDS is running: ps aux | grep snort
Check rules are loaded: snort -c snort.lua --rule-to-text
Ensure traffic matches rule criteria
Verify log file permissions
Kibana not accessible:

Check service status: sudo systemctl status kibana
Verify port 5601 is listening: netstat -tlnp | grep 5601
Allow time for startup (can take 2-3 minutes)
Conclusion
This lab provided hands-on experience with two leading IDS/IPS platforms: Snort and Suricata. Students learned to install, configure, and customize detection rules using Python scripting. The integration with Elastic Stack demonstrated modern approaches to security event analysis and visualization. By comparing both systems, students gained insight into selecting appropriate IDS solutions for different network environments. These skills are essential for security operations, threat detection, and incident response roles.

Key Takeaways:

IDS/IPS systems require careful tuning to balance detection and false positives
Custom rule development enables detection of organization-specific threats
Log aggregation and visualization are critical for security monitoring
Performance considerations impact IDS deployment decisions
Python automation enhances security operations efficiency
